body of the provided request requires a C++ program to identify unique elements, frequency counts, and the most frequent number. To achieve this, we use an associative container, specifically `std::unordered_map<int, int>`, which provides efficient storage and retrieval for counting occurrences within the array.

The implementation begins by iterating through the static array of integers provided in your query. As we traverse each element, the map automatically tracks the number of times each integer appears, effectively distinguishing between unique elements and repeating values.

After populating the map, we determine the count of unique elements by checking the size of our map container. Simultaneously, we iterate through the stored key-value pairs to isolate those with a frequency greater than one, which defines our non-unique elements.

To identify the highest frequency element, we maintain two variables during our final iteration: one for the maximum count found so far and one for the integer associated with that count. This logic ensures we accurately capture the most recurring value.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>

int main() {
    int arr[] = {1, 5, 3, 6, 2, 3, 5, 8, 10, 5, 1, 2, 3, 6, 1, 10};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    std::unordered_map<int, int> freq;
    for(int i : arr) freq[i]++;
    int maxFreq = 0, mostFreq = -1;
    for(auto const& [val, count] : freq) {
        if(count > maxFreq) { maxFreq = count; mostFreq = val; }
    }
    std::cout << "Unique elements: " << freq.size() << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Most frequent: " << mostFreq << " (" << maxFreq << " times)" << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

